  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🐩 Dog Grooming Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a grooming salon, a home studio or a van, from Wash & Wag to The Pampered Pooch."
    },
    "intro": "Good dog grooming names are friendly, a little playful and say what you do: <em>Groom With a View</em>, <em>Bubbles &amp; Pups</em> or <em>The Posh Pooch</em>. A grooming name goes on the shop front, the van and every booking reminder, so it should sound friendly and say what you do. This **Dog Grooming Name Generator** has names in eight styles, including mobile grooming and names built from your own name or town.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good dog grooming names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Groom With a View</strong>: a pun on the film title that owners will repeat.</li><li><strong>Ruff Around the Edges</strong>: funny, and honest about how dogs arrive.</li><li><strong>Bubbles &amp; Pups</strong>: cute and clear, with the bath in the name.</li><li><strong>Lather Up Pups</strong>: catchy, with a word that sounds like a good wash.</li><li><strong>The Posh Pooch</strong>: a classic for a salon with a smart shop front.</li><li><strong>Coat &amp; Co.</strong>: short and modern, and it works on a van.</li><li><strong>Pups on Wheels</strong>: says you come to the customer.</li><li><strong>Grooming by Sarah</strong>: your own name, for a one-person home studio.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What owners look for in a grooming name",
        "content": "<p>Owners want a groomer who is gentle, tidy and trustworthy with their dog, so the best names balance fun with care.</p><ul><li><strong>Bath and coat words</strong> make the job clear: <em>suds</em>, <em>lather</em>, <em>bubbles</em>, <em>fluff</em>, <em>coat</em>, <em>trim</em>.</li><li><strong>Dog words</strong> make it friendly: <em>pups</em>, <em>paws</em>, <em>hound</em>, <em>pooch</em>, <em>wag</em>.</li><li><strong>Puns</strong> stick in the memory, but keep one per name: <em>From Hairy to Handsome</em> works because it's a story, not just a joke.</li><li><strong>Spa, Boutique and Lounge</strong> say you charge a little more: <em>Kensington Dog Spa</em>, <em>Glossy Spaniel Grooming Boutique</em>.</li></ul><p>If you're mobile, say so in the name. <em>on Wheels</em>, <em>Mobile</em> or <em>on the Move</em> tells people what the van is for before they read the phone number.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>Every name here has a <strong>Check</strong> button, which opens the <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a>: it looks up domains, social handles, the company register and trade mark searches in one place. Before you order signs or cards:</p><ul><li><strong>Company register.</strong> In the UK, search Companies House; a limited company's name can't be the same as one already registered. In the US, search your state's business register.</li><li><strong>Trade marks.</strong> Search the UK Intellectual Property Office or the USPTO for marks in your line of work. Registering a company doesn't give you trade mark rights.</li><li><strong>Domain and handles.</strong> Try the .co.uk or .com and the Instagram and Facebook names at the same time, and change the name slightly rather than settle for a messy handle.</li><li><strong>Local search.</strong> Look up the name with your town. Two similar businesses a few miles apart confuse customers and reviews.</li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For the full process, read <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p><p>For the rest of the pet trade, try the <a href=\"dog-walking-name-generator.html\">Dog Walking</a> and <a href=\"pet-sitting-name-generator.html\">Pet Sitting</a> name generators.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_care-names.html\">pet, care and learning business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What are good dog grooming business names?",
        "answer": "Friendly names that say what you do, like Bubbles & Pups, Groom With a View or The Posh Pooch. Bath words (suds, lather, fluff) and dog words (paws, pups, hound) make the job clear at a glance."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a good name for a mobile dog grooming business?",
        "answer": "Say that you come to the customer: words like on Wheels, on the Go, Mobile or Van tell people at a glance, and the name reads well on the side of the van. Pick the Mobile grooming style for ideas such as Pups on Wheels or Lather on the Move."
      },
      {
        "question": "Are funny dog grooming names a good idea?",
        "answer": "Yes, if the joke is kind. Names like Ruff Around the Edges or From Grubby to Sparkling make owners smile and remember you; avoid anything that makes the dogs sound neglected."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a dog grooming name is free?",
        "answer": "Press Check on any name to see whether it's free as a company name, a domain and a social media handle. Before you commit, search the official registers too: in the UK, Companies House; in the US, your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search."
      }
    ]
  }
